WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.680
Here's a puzzle.

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:05.740
The Federal Reserve sets one interest
rate: the overnight rate banks charge each

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:10.860
other. It does not set the yield on a
10-year Treasury note, or a 30-year bond,

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:11.840
or your mortgage.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:15.420
Those are set by buyers and sellers in a
market.

00:00:15.500 --> 00:00:19.300
So it's entirely possible for long-term
rates to climb while the Fed is telling

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:20.480
everyone to relax.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.980
The week of October 5th, 2026 is a clean
example.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.920
The week before, senior Fed officials had
talked down an October rate hike,

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:34.300
and Nick Timiraos of the Wall Street
Journal noted the market's odds of one

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.260
fell from around 70 percent to around 25.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:44.600
Yet on Wednesday, October 7th, the 10-year
yield touched 5.364 percent during the

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:51.100
day, a 24-year high. The 30-year sat
around 5.7 percent, a range not seen since

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:57.160
about 2002. The Friday before, Treasury
Secretary Scott Bessent had said, "I can't

00:00:57.160 --> 00:00:58.100
control the bond market."

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:01.420
So what does control it?

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:05.900
By the end of this lesson you'll have four
questions to ask any time long rates jump.

00:01:06.620 --> 00:01:10.180
We'll build them one at a time, starting
with what a long yield actually is.

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.190
Picture a 10-year yield as one bar split
into two blocks.

00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:18.550
The bottom block is the expected average
short rate.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:21.010
The top block is the term premium.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.170
Start with the bottom block.

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.550
Say you have money to park for ten years.

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:30.630
You could buy a 10-year note, or you could
lend overnight and roll that loan every

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:35.950
day for a decade. If the two plans are
expected to pay about the same, the

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.870
10-year yield should roughly equal the
average overnight rate you expect over

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:40.670
those ten years.

00:01:41.590 --> 00:01:46.110
That's why the 10-year cares about the
Fed, but about the Fed's whole path, not

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:46.990
its next meeting.

00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:50.350
What moves this block is inflation,

00:01:51.190 --> 00:01:55.370
growth, and anything that changes where
the Fed is expected to take rates and how

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:57.870
long it stays there. Now the top block.

00:01:58.930 --> 00:02:02.130
Locking up money for ten years is riskier
than rolling it overnight.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:06.290
If rates rise, your note's price falls and
you're stuck with it.

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:11.390
So investors want extra pay for holding
long, and that extra is the term premium.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.650
It rises with things that make holding
long feel riskier, or that need more

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:21.430
buyers to absorb: heavy government
borrowing, big deficits, uncertainty about

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.290
inflation or policy, and fewer dependable
buyers.

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:29.450
You can't observe it directly; it's
estimated with models, and none of this

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:32.750
week's sources gives an estimate, so I
won't put a number on it.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.410
Here's the worked example.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:38.470
On October 7th the Fed released minutes
from its September meeting.

00:02:39.730 --> 00:02:44.270
Officials attributed the rise in yields to
expected Fed hikes, the AI build-out, and

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:49.390
solid growth. Fed staff added that some of
the surge may reflect "uncertainty related

00:02:49.390 --> 00:02:53.350
to the U.S. Treasury's announcement and
implementation of the buyback program."

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:55.790
Let's sort those onto the bar.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:03:00.530
Expected hikes go in the bottom block;
that's literally the expected path.

00:03:01.490 --> 00:03:05.490
Solid growth goes there too, because a
strong economy keeps the Fed's path

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:11.090
higher. The AI build-out straddles both:
growth is bottom block, borrowing is top

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:11.370
block.

00:03:12.350 --> 00:03:14.170
Buyback uncertainty is pure top block.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:18.350
It doesn't change where the Fed is going,
only how comfortable people feel holding

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.110
long bonds.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:21.370
Michael Cembalest of J.P.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:25.770
Morgan offered his own list in his October
8th Eye on the Market: "sticky inflation,

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:31.590
hyperscaler debt issuance and US GDP
growth above expectations." He added that

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:37.970
"for the first time since 1970, the US
budget deficit is worse than 5% of GDP and

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:40.730
deteriorating during a period of economic
expansion."

00:03:42.010 --> 00:03:44.490
Sticky inflation and strong growth go in
the bottom block.

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:49.210
Hyperscaler debt, meaning the giant cloud
companies borrowing to build data centers,

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:54.350
and a big deficit go in the top block:
more bonds competing for the same buyers.

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:58.430
And notice what talking down October did
and didn't change.

00:03:58.730 --> 00:04:02.870
John Authers of Bloomberg Opinion pointed
out that futures still priced the fed

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:08.310
funds rate at 4.75 percent a year out, and
the minutes said most officials still saw

00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.670
another hike as likely by year end.

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.250
The Fed slowed the pace, not the
destination.

00:04:15.250 --> 00:04:19.570
A 10-year yield averages over a decade, so
the destination is what counts.

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:23.030
That brings us to supply.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:28.030
The U.S. Treasury sells its bonds at
auctions, on a schedule, whatever the

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:31.570
market's mood, and one auction result
tells you a lot about demand.

00:04:32.410 --> 00:04:36.310
Start with the clearing yield, called the
high yield, and compare it with the

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:40.550
when-issued yield, which is where the same
bond was trading just before the auction.

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.890
If the auction clears below when-issued,
it "stopped through":

00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.790
buyers paid more than the market expected,
which is strong.

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:52.570
If it clears above, it "tailed," which is
weak.

00:04:53.590 --> 00:04:57.550
Remember that bond prices and yields move
in opposite directions, so a lower yield

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.830
means a higher price.

00:04:59.990 --> 00:05:03.110
Next, the bid-to-cover: dollars bid for
every dollar sold.

00:05:03.610 --> 00:05:06.170
Higher is better, and you judge it against
recent auctions.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:08.310
Then, who took it.

00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:12.530
Indirect bidders are the bucket that
includes foreign buyers, among them

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:13.510
foreign central banks.

00:05:14.490 --> 00:05:16.070
Direct bidders buy for their own accounts.

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.830
Primary dealers are the banks expected to
bid at every auction, and they end up

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:22.230
holding whatever nobody else wanted.

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.010
A big dealer share is a warning sign.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.990
Here are this week's two, in that table.

00:05:28.770 --> 00:05:32.790
These figures come through secondary
auction trackers and a relayed Reuters

00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:35.630
report, all citing Treasury's own results.

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:42.390
Column one, the 10-year reopening: 39
billion dollars at 5.300 percent, the

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.290
highest 10-year auction yield since
November 2000.

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:51.890
When-issued was 5.317, so it stopped
through by 1.7 basis points; a basis point

00:05:51.890 --> 00:05:53.810
is a hundredth of a percentage point.

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:58.950
Bid-to-cover 2.77, against a six-auction
average of 2.54.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:05.390
Indirects 80.3 percent, directs 17.1,
dealers 2.5, which Reuters called the

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:08.670
smallest dealer share since the aftermath
of the financial crisis.

00:06:08.930 --> 00:06:10.330
That's a strong auction.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:16.950
Column two, the 30-year reopening: 22
billion dollars at 5.618 percent, against

00:06:16.950 --> 00:06:20.230
5.617 when-issued, essentially right on
it.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:23.830
Bid-to-cover 2.54, against an average of
2.41.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:30.090
Indirects 72.3 percent, directs 20.9,
dealers 6.8, against an average of 10.3.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:34.190
One tracker called it "in line." Decent,
not spectacular.

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:37.750
So in a week of multi-decade highs, buyers
showed up.

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:39.270
They just needed a higher yield.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.890
Why do foreign official holders matter so
much?

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:46.190
Central banks holding Treasuries as
reserves are about the least

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:48.070
price-sensitive buyers there are.

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:53.370
If they step back, the Treasury has to
sell to investors who need a better yield

00:06:53.370 --> 00:06:56.670
to take the bond, and that shows up in the
term premium.

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:01.330
Ray Dalio made exactly that argument on
Bloomberg TV this week:

00:07:02.150 --> 00:07:06.190
"The Chinese don't want to continue to
accumulate," and "when you have a

00:07:06.190 --> 00:07:10.910
debtor-creditor relationship and you have
an adversary relationship, that's a very

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:15.670
difficult dynamic." He said Japan has lent
"a lot of money" it now wants back.

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.510
That's his view. The holdings figures we
have come from a secondary relay:

00:07:20.450 --> 00:07:26.710
China around 618 billion dollars as of
July, and Japan at 1.1 trillion, down 12.8

00:07:26.710 --> 00:07:26.950
billion.

00:07:29.650 --> 00:07:32.310
Everything so far explains which way long
rates go.

00:07:33.290 --> 00:07:34.490
This section, the plumbing,

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:36.470
explains why they overshoot.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:40.250
Many big investors don't hold their bond
exposure as bonds.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:41.930
They hold Treasury futures,

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.870
contracts to deliver a bond at a set price
on a future date.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:50.930
Futures are cheap to hold and easy to
trade, so asset managers use them to hit a

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:51.390
target duration.

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:56.450
Duration measures how much a bond's price
moves when yields change; longer bonds

00:07:56.450 --> 00:07:57.110
have more of it.

00:07:57.970 --> 00:08:01.930
Here's the twist. A bond futures contract
doesn't name a single bond.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.350
Whoever is short the contract can deliver
any bond from a basket, and naturally

00:08:07.350 --> 00:08:08.610
delivers the cheapest one.

00:08:09.430 --> 00:08:13.330
That's the cheapest-to-deliver bond, and
the future's price tracks it.

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:15.370
Read the chain from the left.

00:08:16.530 --> 00:08:19.870
Matt Levine, in his Money Stuff
newsletter, walked through the long-bond

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:25.230
contract. It's priced off a fictitious
20-year Treasury paying 6 percent, and

00:08:25.230 --> 00:08:28.590
bonds with 15 to just under 25 years left
can be delivered.

00:08:30.190 --> 00:08:35.130
Which end of that basket is cheapest
depends on where yields sit relative to 6

00:08:35.130 --> 00:08:39.870
percent. In his words: "When long-term
Treasury interest rates are lower than 6%,

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.630
it is a 15-year Treasury futures contract.

00:08:43.290 --> 00:08:48.170
When long-term Treasury interest rates are
higher than 6%, it is a 25-year Treasury

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.870
futures contract." Now the feedback loop.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:55.300
Bloomberg reported that the current
cheapest-to-deliver is the 2.5 percent

00:08:55.470 --> 00:09:00.510
February 2045 bond, and sketched a
scenario where a 30-year yield near 6

00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:02.930
percent switches it to a 2050 maturity.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:08.690
Then each contract carries more duration,
so an asset manager targeting a fixed

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:11.490
duration suddenly has too much and sells
futures.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:16.870
BNP Paribas strategists said that "could
exacerbate the rise in long-end yields."

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:23.050
Levine adds a step: futures selling could
push hedge funds to unwind basis trades,

00:09:23.050 --> 00:09:26.750
where they own the bond and are short the
future, by selling bonds.

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.450
Selling pushes yields up, which brings the
switch closer.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:35.770
His caveat: managers would hold fewer
contracts after a switch, so total

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:38.370
duration might not jump much, and could
even fall.

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:40.890
It's a scenario, not a forecast.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:43.830
Second amplifier: crowded positioning.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.830
In a Bloomberg story we have only through
a secondary relay, Citi's David Bieber put

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:53.050
bond positioning at "extreme short"
levels, and Bank of America said short

00:09:53.050 --> 00:09:56.510
positions held by trend-following funds
"remain near maximum levels."

00:09:57.530 --> 00:09:58.570
Crowding cuts both ways.

00:09:59.230 --> 00:10:03.690
When everyone is already short, bad news
pushes yields up fast, and good news

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:06.090
triggers sharp snapbacks as shorts buy
back.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:08.870
That fits this week's path:

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:14.790
5.364 intraday on Wednesday, settled
around 5.28, back to 5.35 on Thursday

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:17.210
morning, and lower again that afternoon.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:19.730
Third: convexity hedging.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:23.290
Some bond holdings get longer in duration
as rates rise.

00:10:24.330 --> 00:10:28.970
The classic case is mortgage bonds: when
rates rise, fewer homeowners refinance, so

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:32.050
the bonds pay back more slowly and act
like longer bonds.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:37.150
Holders rehedge by selling Treasuries or
futures, which pushes rates higher still.

00:10:37.850 --> 00:10:42.150
The same Bloomberg story named convexity
hedging and futures mechanics among the

00:10:42.150 --> 00:10:45.250
forces making this move worse, without
sizing either.

00:10:45.250 --> 00:10:49.890
And BNP noted that long-end futures carry
their own "negative convexity profile"

00:10:49.890 --> 00:10:53.610
that "can induce further duration selling
during selloffs."

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:55.690
None of these sets the direction.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.070
They set the speed, and the overshoot.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:02.110
Why should anyone outside a trading desk
care?

00:11:03.150 --> 00:11:06.270
The 10-year anchors the biggest loan most
families ever take.

00:11:07.430 --> 00:11:08.110
Follow the arrows.

00:11:08.970 --> 00:11:12.950
U.S. mortgage rates are priced off the
10-year plus a spread for the lender's

00:11:12.950 --> 00:11:16.950
costs and risks, so when the 10-year
rises, mortgage rates follow.

00:11:18.190 --> 00:11:23.390
Freddie Mac's weekly survey put the
30-year fixed at 7.40 percent, up from

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:26.750
7.28 a week earlier and 6.30 a year ago.

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:31.870
The Mortgage Bankers Association's
measure, a different survey, hit 7.49

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:34.310
percent, a three-year high, up from 7.3.

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:37.430
Two surveys, two numbers, so always name
the source.

00:11:38.350 --> 00:11:39.570
Next arrow, home buying.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:44.310
A higher rate means a bigger monthly
payment on the same house, so buyers

00:11:44.310 --> 00:11:48.030
qualify for less, and builders pay more to
finance what they build.

00:11:48.810 --> 00:11:53.070
Timiraos noted that MBA purchase
applications were down 13 percent from a

00:11:53.070 --> 00:11:54.970
year earlier on a four-week rolling
average.

00:11:56.050 --> 00:12:01.550
Then rents. Torsten Slok at Apollo calls
this the "Higher Rates, Higher Rent Doom

00:12:01.550 --> 00:12:04.110
Loop." High rates mean builders build
less.

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.270
Less building means rents rise.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:10.830
Higher rents push inflation up, and higher
inflation keeps rates high.

00:12:11.510 --> 00:12:13.110
That last step is the dashed arrow.

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:17.150
It matters because owners' equivalent
rent, the housing cost measure in the

00:12:17.150 --> 00:12:19.790
consumer price index, is about a quarter
of the basket.

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:25.690
That's Slok's view. In fairness, he also
listed higher rates biting on housing and

00:12:25.690 --> 00:12:30.410
autos as one reason rates could peak: the
same chain can slow the economy down.

00:12:32.590 --> 00:12:36.810
So next time long yields jump, don't stop
at "what did the Fed do?" Ask four

00:12:36.810 --> 00:12:39.110
questions. One: which part moved?

00:12:39.650 --> 00:12:43.290
Is the expected path rising, because of
inflation, growth or the Fed's

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:48.090
destination? Or is the term premium
rising, because of supply, deficits or

00:12:48.090 --> 00:12:52.410
uncertainty? Fed minutes and futures
pricing speak to the first; the second is

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:53.290
much of what's left.

00:12:54.250 --> 00:12:56.010
Two: how did the auctions go?

00:12:56.430 --> 00:12:59.190
Did the sale tail or stop through against
when-issued?

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:01.710
Was the bid-to-cover strong against its
average?

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:06.310
Did indirect and direct buyers take the
bonds, or were dealers left with more?

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:09.050
Three: is the plumbing adding to it?

00:13:09.650 --> 00:13:12.570
Is the cheapest-to-deliver bond about to
change?

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:18.470
Is duration extending? Are shorts crowded,
and are convexity hedges adding sales to

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:19.610
an already falling market?

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:22.250
Four: are mortgages following?

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:24.370
Are mortgage rates moving with Treasuries?

00:13:24.930 --> 00:13:27.530
Are purchase applications and construction
weakening?

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:29.830
Could less building put later pressure on
rents?

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:32.770
The checklist won't tell you where yields
go next.

00:13:34.030 --> 00:13:38.870
Our week had a strong 10-year auction,
extreme short positioning, a Fed slowing

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:43.990
its pace but not its destination, and
mortgage rates at multi-year highs, all at

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:46.510
once. A checklist doesn't hand you a
verdict.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:49.330
It tells you which part of the machine is
doing the work.

